ction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 14.3, "y": 25.2, "width": 71.4, "height": 25.3, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 14.3, "y": 25.2, "width": 71.4, "height": 25.3, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 14.3, "y": 25.2, "width": 71.4, "height": 25.3, "rotation": 0, "text": ["Let $( p ^ { 1 } , x ^ { 1 } )$ and $( p ^ { 2 } , x ^ { 2 } )$ be a pair of observations on prices and consumption bundles, with $p ^ { \\prime } \\gg 0$ and $x ^ { t } \\geq 0$ for $t = 1 , 2$ and such that $( p ^ { 1 } , x ^ { 1 } ) \\neq ( p ^ { 2 } , x ^ { 2 } ) .$ We say that individual choices (given by x) satisfy the Weak Axiom of Revealed Preference if, whenever $p ^ { 2 } x ^ { 1 } < p ^ { 2 } x ^ { 2 } ,$ we must then have that p1 x\u00b2 > p1x1. "]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 14.3, "y": 54.1, "width": 70.8, "height": 39.5, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 14.3, "y": 54.1, "width": 70.8, "height": 39.5, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 14.3, "y": 54.1, "width": 70.8, "height": 39.5, "rotation": 0, "text": ["The idea of this definition is fairly intuitive: take the price vector $p ^ { 2 }$ ;at those prices, the consumer chooses the bundle $x ^ { 2 }$ However, $x ^ { 1 }$ was also available at those prices (i.e. $p ^ { 2 } x ^ { 1 } \\leq p ^ { 2 } x ^ { 2 } ) ,$ but was not chosen. This implies that the consumer preferred $x ^ { 2 }$ over $x ^ { \\uparrow }$ (so, she revealed her preference). Therefore, it must be the case that when prices were $p ^ { \\uparrow }$ , $x ^ { 2 }$ could not be available for consumption, because if that were the case, then she would again choose $x ^ { 2 }$ instead of $x ^ { 1 } .$ Figure 4 illustrates an example in which the WARP is NOT satisfied. "]}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}], "created_username": "10. NAIST nlp_annot_10@naist.ac.jp, 16", "created_ago": "0\u00a0minutes", "completed_by": {"id": 16, "first_name": "10. NAIST", "last_name": "", "email": "nlp_annot_10@naist.ac.jp"}, "task": {"id": 138081, "data": {"ocr": "/lectrans/data/local-files/?d=/large/datas/frederikus-h/lectrans-testset/25.10.annot_tools/label-studio/ocr_check_storage_source/lec_slides/intmd_microeconomics/Dqko567cE9I/24.Dqko567cE9I-Scene-191-01.jpg", "text": "/lectrans/data/local-files/?d=/large/datas/frederikus-h/lectrans-testset/25.10.annot_tools/label-studio/ocr_check_storage_source/gpt_41mini_outs/intmd_microeconomics/Dqko567cE9I/24.Dqko567cE9I-Scene-191-01.txt"}, "meta": {}, "created_at": "2025-11-01T12:32:23.464241Z", "updated_at": "2025-11-01T12:32:23.473867Z", "is_labeled": true, "overlap": 1, "inner_id": 194, "total_annotations": 1, "cancelled_annotations": 0, "total_predictions": 1, "comment_count": 0, "unresolved_comment_count": 0, "last_comment_updated_at": null, "project": 29, "updated_by": 1, "file_upload": null, "comment_authors": []}, "was_cancelled": false, "ground_truth": false, "created_at": "2025-11-20T07:05:31.659743Z", "updated_at": "2025-11-20T07:05:31.659769Z", "draft_created_at": null, "lead_time": 3.628, "import_id": 